course: dictionary
lesson: 8938
title: Silage: food kept for winter
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words name things from farm life. Read the words around them, because they are the context and they leave clues. «Silage is grass wrapped up airtight so bacteria turn its sugars into acid and pickle it, keeping it fresh for months, and cows love it.» Airtight, pickle, fresh for months and cows love it all tell you what silage is for.
explain: Silage is made from grass or maize that is cut, packed tightly and kept without air. Farmers store it so cows and sheep have food in winter. «In winter many cows live indoors eating hay and silage because fields are muddy and grass hardly grows.»
explain: Hay and silage are both winter food, but they are made in different ways. «In June the grass is cut, dried in the sun, turned and baled as hay, or wrapped airtight in black plastic so it pickles into silage.» Hay is dried in the sun, but silage is sealed up so it pickles.
explain: Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then swap your meaning into the sentence. «In winter the cows live in big sheds, eating the hay and silage saved in summer.» If silage means stored green food for cows, the sentence still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
fact: Silage means grass, maize or other green crops that are cut, packed tightly and kept without air so they ferment.
fact: Farmers make silage to feed cows and sheep in winter.
fact: Silage can be kept in a clamp, a pit, a silo or in big black plastic-wrapped bales.
fact: Bacteria pickle the crop, so silage stays fresh for months.
fact: «Silage» is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some silage, not two silages.
exercise: Silage is grass wrapped up airtight so bacteria pickle it. What is silage? | A kind of cow shed | A tall tower for milk | *Green crops, cut and packed tightly without air, to feed cows and sheep in winter | Dried hay, baled in the sun in June
exercise: Why do farmers make silage? | *To feed cows and sheep in winter | To make the fields muddy | To sell it as a summer sweet
exercise: What does airtight mean in «wrapped up airtight»? | Open at the top | *Sealed so no air can get in | Letting air in and out freely | Very warm and damp
test: What do bacteria do to silage? | Dry it out in the sun | Freeze it until spring | *Turn its sugars into acid, so it pickles
test: Silage is a noun. Is it countable? | Only in plural, like scissors | Yes, you say two silages | *No, it is uncountable: you say some silage
exam: Which is the best reason silage keeps food for cows in winter? | It is frozen in the snow | *It is kept airtight so bacteria pickle it and it stays fresh for months | It is sold in shops in summer | It is dried in the sun until it crackles
